1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:03,022
Cocoa is less than half its peak,

2
00:00:03,022 --> 00:00:04,654
yet chocolate isn't cheaper,

3
00:00:04,654 --> 00:00:07,450
because Hershey is keeping the prices it raised.

4
00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:11,050
That's how it sold less candy and still doubled earnings per share.

5
00:00:11,850 --> 00:00:15,390
In the third quarter Hershey sold about 10% less,

6
00:00:15,390 --> 00:00:17,846
but adjusted EPS hit 2.64 dollars,

7
00:00:17,846 --> 00:00:19,580
up 103% from a year ago.

8
00:00:20,380 --> 00:00:21,864
But here's the odd part. Cocoa,

9
00:00:21,864 --> 00:00:23,300
the raw material of chocolate,

10
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:24,880
is down nearly 60% from its peak.

11
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:27,920
So why didn't chocolate get cheaper?

12
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:29,942
The day before Halloween,

13
00:00:29,942 --> 00:00:33,023
let's open the notebook on the price tag inside the candy bowl.

14
00:00:33,023 --> 00:00:35,370
At the end: three numbers to watch next quarter.

15
00:00:37,070 --> 00:00:39,460
Chapter one: the money spent on Halloween.

16
00:00:40,260 --> 00:00:42,670
The National Retail Federation expects

17
00:00:42,670 --> 00:00:46,603
Americans to spend a record 13.5 billion dollars on Halloween,

18
00:00:46,603 --> 00:00:49,330
topping last year's record of 13.1 billion.

19
00:00:49,530 --> 00:00:51,614
That's 115 dollars per person,

20
00:00:51,614 --> 00:00:54,670
and nearly three in four people taking part.

21
00:00:55,460 --> 00:00:58,232
Where does it go? 4.4 billion on costumes,

22
00:00:58,232 --> 00:01:00,014
4.3 billion on decorations,

23
00:01:00,014 --> 00:01:01,400
4.1 billion on candy.

24
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:03,257
Candy is the symbol of Halloween,

25
00:01:03,257 --> 00:01:05,667
yet more money goes to costumes and decorations.

26
00:01:05,667 --> 00:01:06,270
First twist.

27
00:01:07,070 --> 00:01:10,633
Split the 4.4 billion: adults 2 billion,

28
00:01:10,633 --> 00:01:14,106
kids 1.5 billion, and pets 920 million.

29
00:01:14,106 --> 00:01:17,580
Top pet costume: pumpkin, then hot dog.

30
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:20,532
Whether the dogs wanted to be hot dogs,

31
00:01:20,532 --> 00:01:21,450
nobody asked.

32
00:01:22,260 --> 00:01:25,553
Where they shop: discount stores 39%,

33
00:01:25,553 --> 00:01:29,380
Halloween specialty stores 32%, online 27%.

34
00:01:29,580 --> 00:01:32,658
And half start shopping in September or earlier.

35
00:01:32,658 --> 00:01:35,800
Halloween isn't one day; it's a two-month season.

36
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:39,977
One surprise: the share planning to buy

37
00:01:39,977 --> 00:01:43,180
Halloween cards rose from 38% to 42%.

38
00:01:43,380 --> 00:01:45,271
Sending feelings instead of sweets?

39
00:01:45,271 --> 00:01:47,270
Cards alone take 800 million dollars.

40
00:01:48,070 --> 00:01:50,044
But candy moves the other way.

41
00:01:50,044 --> 00:01:51,755
NielsenIQ data showed U.S.

42
00:01:51,755 --> 00:01:55,900
candy sales down 11.7% from a year earlier as of mid-September.

43
00:01:56,100 --> 00:01:57,012
Less candy sold,

44
00:01:57,012 --> 00:01:59,007
more money made by the candy maker.

45
00:01:59,007 --> 00:02:00,660
That's today's real question.

46
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:04,224
Chapter two: the winter of 2024.

47
00:02:04,224 --> 00:02:06,029
A poor harvest hit West Africa,

48
00:02:06,029 --> 00:02:08,650
where more than half the world's cocoa grows.

49
00:02:08,850 --> 00:02:09,661
Extreme weather,

50
00:02:09,661 --> 00:02:12,450
a tree-killing disease and aging farms all hit at once.

51
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:14,917
On December 18, 2024,

52
00:02:14,917 --> 00:02:18,352
a ton of cocoa hit 12,931 dollars intraday,

53
00:02:18,352 --> 00:02:19,710
an all-time high.

54
00:02:19,910 --> 00:02:22,127
A year later it was in the 6,000s,

55
00:02:22,127 --> 00:02:24,735
and early this month it's in the 5,000s,

56
00:02:24,735 --> 00:02:26,300
less than half the peak.

57
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:29,625
Supply came back: by early October Ivory Coast

58
00:02:29,625 --> 00:02:32,615
shipments were up 19.8% from a year earlier,

59
00:02:32,615 --> 00:02:35,400
and exchange stocks hit a 2.25-year high.

60
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:39,157
Hershey took that cost bomb head-on.

61
00:02:39,157 --> 00:02:43,840
A year ago its adjusted gross margin had fallen to 31.8%.

62
00:02:44,040 --> 00:02:46,195
For every 100 dollars of sales,

63
00:02:46,195 --> 00:02:48,906
about 32 dollars were left after costs.

64
00:02:48,906 --> 00:02:51,200
So Hershey chose to raise prices.

65
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:56,855
Chapter three: the report card. On October 29,

66
00:02:56,855 --> 00:03:00,723
higher prices added about 11 points to sales,

67
00:03:00,723 --> 00:03:03,990
while lower volume took away about 10.

68
00:03:04,790 --> 00:03:07,745
If a family that bought ten bags now buys nine,

69
00:03:07,745 --> 00:03:09,505
but each bag costs 11% more,

70
00:03:09,505 --> 00:03:11,140
sales stay about the same.

71
00:03:11,340 --> 00:03:16,094
Hershey's sales actually rose 3% to 3.28 billion dollars.

72
00:03:16,094 --> 00:03:18,430
Less sold, yet more revenue.

73
00:03:19,230 --> 00:03:22,437
But the money left over changed dramatically.

74
00:03:22,437 --> 00:03:24,718
Adjusted gross margin hit 42.7%,

75
00:03:24,718 --> 00:03:26,500
up 10.9 points in a year.

76
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:31,650
A company that kept 32 dollars per 100 now keeps 43.

77
00:03:32,450 --> 00:03:34,805
In North America Confectionery,

78
00:03:34,805 --> 00:03:36,629
home of Halloween candy,

79
00:03:36,629 --> 00:03:40,124
it's even starker: about 13 points from price,

80
00:03:40,124 --> 00:03:41,720
volume down about 11.

81
00:03:41,920 --> 00:03:43,960
Fewer shoppers at the candy shelf,

82
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:45,580
bigger numbers on the tags.

83
00:03:46,390 --> 00:03:49,154
So adjusted EPS doubled to 2.64 dollars.

84
00:03:49,154 --> 00:03:53,300
It includes about 22 cents of tariff refunds received in Q3,

85
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:56,340
but even without them it rose more than 80%.

86
00:03:56,530 --> 00:03:59,250
That's how selling less and earning more works.

87
00:04:00,050 --> 00:04:03,147
Hershey's CEO said the results came even as

88
00:04:03,147 --> 00:04:05,236
consumers grow more cautious.

89
00:04:05,236 --> 00:04:08,477
Prices went up while wallets were tightening,

90
00:04:08,477 --> 00:04:09,990
and the company held.

91
00:04:11,690 --> 00:04:14,162
Chapter four, back to the first question.

92
00:04:14,162 --> 00:04:15,910
Cocoa fell by more than half.

93
00:04:15,910 --> 00:04:18,080
Why isn't chocolate getting cheaper?

94
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:22,151
There's a saying: prices take the elevator up when costs rise,

95
00:04:22,151 --> 00:04:24,050
and the stairs down when costs fall.

96
00:04:24,840 --> 00:04:26,316
Three reasons. First,

97
00:04:26,316 --> 00:04:28,706
chocolate makers buy cocoa months,

98
00:04:28,706 --> 00:04:31,447
sometimes more than a year, in advance.

99
00:04:31,447 --> 00:04:34,610
Today's cheap cocoa reaches costs only later.

100
00:04:34,820 --> 00:04:37,788
Second, cost isn't just cocoa: sugar, milk,

101
00:04:37,788 --> 00:04:40,410
packaging, labor and freight move too.

102
00:04:40,610 --> 00:04:41,021
Third,

103
00:04:41,021 --> 00:04:45,340
cutting a price you've raised is the last card a company plays.

104
00:04:46,140 --> 00:04:49,345
So how does Hershey raise prices and hold on?

105
00:04:49,345 --> 00:04:52,693
The answer is in the names: Hershey's, Reese's,

106
00:04:52,693 --> 00:04:55,400
Kisses, and in the U.S., even Kit Kat.

107
00:04:55,600 --> 00:04:58,373
The names kids grab first from the Halloween bowl.

108
00:04:58,373 --> 00:05:01,091
As long as shoppers pick the name over the price,

109
00:05:01,091 --> 00:05:02,700
the company can raise prices.

110
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:06,687
Not everyone took the same road. Mondelez,

111
00:05:06,687 --> 00:05:07,826
the Oreo maker,

112
00:05:07,826 --> 00:05:11,848
reported the same day: pricing added only 0.3 points,

113
00:05:11,848 --> 00:05:13,290
volume and mix 1.8.

114
00:05:13,490 --> 00:05:15,501
One wins on price, the other on volume.

115
00:05:15,501 --> 00:05:18,130
The snack aisle is running two experiments at once.

116
00:05:18,930 --> 00:05:22,331
Shoppers fight back. In the month to October 17,

117
00:05:22,331 --> 00:05:23,110
U.S. candy,

118
00:05:23,110 --> 00:05:27,290
gum and chocolate sales were down 6.7% from a year earlier.

119
00:05:27,490 --> 00:05:28,841
In the NRF survey,

120
00:05:28,841 --> 00:05:33,120
34% said higher prices would push them to reusable items,

121
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:34,772
31% to compare prices.

122
00:05:34,772 --> 00:05:38,450
Bringing them back is the candy makers' next job.

123
00:05:40,150 --> 00:05:43,005
Chapter five: a store that opens two months a year.

124
00:05:43,005 --> 00:05:46,645
Spirit Halloween is opening about 1,500 pop-up stores in the U.S.

125
00:05:46,645 --> 00:05:50,340
and Canada this year and hiring more than 52,000 seasonal workers.

126
00:05:51,130 --> 00:05:52,657
The trick is empty storefronts.

127
00:05:52,657 --> 00:05:54,626
Rent a big vacant store at summer's end,

128
00:05:54,626 --> 00:05:56,350
open through Halloween, then close.

129
00:05:56,560 --> 00:05:59,565
A year's business packed into two months.

130
00:05:59,565 --> 00:06:03,670
Shoppers starting in September is this store's calendar.

131
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:05,798
Meanwhile Party City,

132
00:06:05,798 --> 00:06:07,837
which sold party goods all year,

133
00:06:07,837 --> 00:06:10,831
filed for bankruptcy in December 2024 and moved

134
00:06:10,831 --> 00:06:12,870
to close its roughly 700 stores.

135
00:06:13,070 --> 00:06:17,819
Same Halloween: the all-season store couldn't hold on,

136
00:06:17,819 --> 00:06:20,370
the two-month store survived.

137
00:06:22,070 --> 00:06:24,163
Chapter six: October 31 in Korea.

138
00:06:24,163 --> 00:06:26,129
Since the 2022 Itaewon tragedy,

139
00:06:26,129 --> 00:06:30,124
Korean retailers have kept Halloween low-key for a fourth year.

140
00:06:30,124 --> 00:06:32,470
Yesterday was the fourth anniversary.

141
00:06:33,270 --> 00:06:37,053
Korean convenience stores have a different autumn peak: Pepero Day,

142
00:06:37,053 --> 00:06:37,730
November 11.

143
00:06:37,930 --> 00:06:44,510
GS25's Pepero Day sales rose 39.2% in 2024 and 30.5% in 2025.

144
00:06:45,310 --> 00:06:49,220
Seoul is again managing crowds at 14 locations

145
00:06:49,220 --> 00:06:51,770
from October 23 to November 1.

146
00:06:51,770 --> 00:06:55,170
If you're celebrating, please stay safe.

147
00:06:56,870 --> 00:06:58,620
Now the three numbers I promised.

148
00:06:58,620 --> 00:07:00,370
Watch these at the next earnings.

149
00:07:01,170 --> 00:07:04,463
One: Hershey's volume, down about 10% this time.

150
00:07:04,463 --> 00:07:07,482
The key is whether it returns near zero once

151
00:07:07,482 --> 00:07:09,540
the price hikes cycle through.

152
00:07:09,740 --> 00:07:13,870
If volume drops sharply again in the Q4 report early next year,

153
00:07:13,870 --> 00:07:15,640
pricing power is weakening.

154
00:07:16,440 --> 00:07:19,625
Two: cocoa futures, now in the 5,000s.

155
00:07:19,625 --> 00:07:23,396
Some forecast El Niño could cut Ivory Coast's

156
00:07:23,396 --> 00:07:25,240
next main crop by 17%.

157
00:07:25,430 --> 00:07:26,550
If cocoa climbs again,

158
00:07:26,550 --> 00:07:28,893
forget cheaper chocolate; if it keeps falling,

159
00:07:28,893 --> 00:07:31,490
Hershey's margins could improve for a while longer.

160
00:07:32,290 --> 00:07:34,022
Three: price or volume.

161
00:07:34,022 --> 00:07:37,259
Hershey trimmed its full-year organic sales

162
00:07:37,259 --> 00:07:40,722
growth outlook to about 3% and raised adjusted

163
00:07:40,722 --> 00:07:42,680
EPS growth to 36 to 38.5%.

164
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:45,667
Confident in pricing, cautious on shoppers.

165
00:07:45,667 --> 00:07:46,315
Next time,

166
00:07:46,315 --> 00:07:49,232
put the two companies' volume numbers side by

167
00:07:49,232 --> 00:07:53,380
side; the key is whether Hershey gets out of negative territory.

168
00:07:54,190 --> 00:07:57,641
No one knows this power better than Warren Buffett.

169
00:07:57,641 --> 00:08:00,888
In 1972 Berkshire Hathaway bought See's Candies,

170
00:08:00,888 --> 00:08:02,715
a California candy company,

171
00:08:02,715 --> 00:08:04,271
for 25 million dollars.

172
00:08:04,271 --> 00:08:08,060
It was earning 5 million pretax on 8 million of capital.

173
00:08:08,260 --> 00:08:09,836
In his 2007 letter,

174
00:08:09,836 --> 00:08:13,487
See's had earned 1.35 billion dollars pretax

175
00:08:13,487 --> 00:08:15,810
over the following 35 years.

176
00:08:16,010 --> 00:08:18,149
That year sales were 383 million,

177
00:08:18,149 --> 00:08:20,677
with only 40 million of capital needed.

178
00:08:20,677 --> 00:08:23,659
The secret of a candy shop earning without big

179
00:08:23,659 --> 00:08:26,770
factories: customers stay even when prices rise.

180
00:08:27,570 --> 00:08:29,361
In one line: when costs fall,

181
00:08:29,361 --> 00:08:30,844
prices come down slowly,

182
00:08:30,844 --> 00:08:34,920
and in between the profit goes to the companies with strong names.

183
00:08:35,120 --> 00:08:37,146
Next time you see a Halloween candy bowl,

184
00:08:37,146 --> 00:08:39,320
think of the structure behind the price tag.

185
00:08:40,120 --> 00:08:42,232
Buffett calls it pricing power,

186
00:08:42,232 --> 00:08:45,910
the single most important thing in judging a business.

187
00:08:46,110 --> 00:08:48,968
I keep numbers like these in a notebook every

188
00:08:48,968 --> 00:08:50,365
day; that's ValueNote.

189
00:08:50,365 --> 00:08:53,350
I'll leave my Hershey notes in the description.

190
00:08:54,150 --> 00:08:56,092
If you want more stories behind the numbers,

191
00:08:56,092 --> 00:08:57,990
please subscribe and turn on notifications.

192
00:08:58,190 --> 00:09:00,420
For the ultimate example of pricing power,

193
00:09:00,420 --> 00:09:03,870
watch the Coca-Cola stock Warren Buffett hasn't sold in 38 years.

194
00:09:04,070 --> 00:09:06,744
Did Halloween candy feel pricier this year?

195
00:09:06,744 --> 00:09:08,920
Tell us in the comments. Thank you.
